---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import { SITE_NAME, TOOLS, withBase } from '../config/site';
---

<Base title="About" description="What TextKit is, how the tools are built, and why everything runs in your browser." path="/about/">
  <div class="prose">
    <h1>About {SITE_NAME}</h1>
    <p>
      TextKit is a small, focused collection of {TOOLS.length} developer and text utilities:
      the tools a developer or writer reaches for several times a day — formatting JSON,
      converting it to CSV, encoding Base64, checking a diff, counting words, generating a
      UUID or a password. Each tool does one job, on one page, with no setup.
    </p>
    <h2>Built differently: your data never moves</h2>
    <p>
      Conventional online tools upload your input to a server, process it there and send the
      result back. TextKit tools are delivered to your browser as JavaScript and run entirely
      on your device. There is no processing server and no upload endpoint, so there is
      nothing to intercept, log or leak — privacy here is a property of the architecture,
      not a promise in a policy document.
    </p>
    <p>
      That architecture has two practical side effects. The tools are instant, because there
      is no network round trip; and they keep working offline once a page has loaded, because
      there is nothing remote left to call.
    </p>
    <h2>Honest limits</h2>
    <p>
      Browser tools are not the right answer for everything. Multi-gigabyte files, heavy
      batch processing and anything that needs a shared team workspace belong in dedicated
      software. TextKit aims squarely at the everyday 95%: a payload to inspect, a string to
      encode, a draft to measure — done in seconds, then the tab closes.
    </p>
    <p>
      New here? Start from the <a href={withBase('/tools/')}>full tool index</a>, or read one
      of the <a href={withBase('/guides/')}>guides</a> on the ideas behind the tools.
    </p>
  </div>
</Base>
